  }))), "Human Response"), mdx("p", null, "I personally would use the PatchTST approach to time series modeling as I believe it is a very innovative approach to how sequential data can be represented, and is one of the driving reasons for my selection of it as the paper. Additionally, I believe there are many alternative approaches to this model that could be utilized for even stronger performance on a broad range of tasks. "), mdx("p", null, "The best scenarios I would apply the model to include time series challenges where there is a substantial amount of training data to utilize, there are complex multi-variate features that contribute to an overall forecast, and the forecasts are required to be long in terms of their context. All of these are where the model performs best as it is designed to capture complex relationships of a series and generate accurate long context forecasts."), mdx("p", null, "More specifically, the domains I would apply this model to would include, climate modeling given there are many different environmental aspects that are all complexly related to one another in producing the current and future states; medical vitals for forecasting patient health, as nuanced trends across different vital signals can produce early signals that are predictive to where general health is trending during a hospital stay; and financial analysis such as forecasting the valuation of a security where the sequential nature of various company measurements may be valuable to obtaining a longer term forecast to effectively manage a client\u2019s portfolio."), mdx("p", null, "Additionally, the prospect of being able to pre-train the model opens many more forecasting opportunities where data may be considerably limited. For example, a restaurant chain may have daily sales data across all its locations. The model could be pre-trained on these various sequences for general patterns and then fine-tuned on a smaller dataset from a new location to obtain the strongest sales forecast possible. "), mdx("br", null), mdx("br", null), mdx("h4", {
